306
02:19:40,219 --> 02:19:45,524
[SPEAKER_06]: It was a tiny red hovel on upper Jerome, a bit run down and rough around the edges.

307
02:19:46,767 --> 02:19:55,170
[SPEAKER_06]: And Mr. Henry seemed so old to us, even then, with a lot of whiskers, impatient and a little scary.

308
02:19:55,890 --> 02:20:00,912
[SPEAKER_06]: One would suspect that he didn't even like kids, but he really must have loved us.

309
02:20:01,812 --> 02:20:06,334
[SPEAKER_06]: Or else, where did all that penny candy come from?

310
02:20:07,314 --> 02:20:08,574
[SPEAKER_06]: He had all of it.

311
02:20:08,935 --> 02:20:09,895
[SPEAKER_06]: No, seriously.

312
02:20:11,030 --> 02:20:16,352
[SPEAKER_06]: We'd bust in there with a few nickels or a handful of pennies, all loud and unruly.

313
02:20:18,273 --> 02:20:21,955
[SPEAKER_06]: He'd hush us up while he finished with grown folks' business.

314
02:20:23,241 --> 02:20:26,763
[SPEAKER_06]: Then he'd be back like a black Willy Wonka up in that old shack.

315
02:20:27,663 --> 02:20:33,006
[SPEAKER_06]: He'd peer over those old horn-rimmed glasses and tell us he didn't have all day.

316
02:20:33,746 --> 02:20:43,551
[SPEAKER_06]: Then he'd blow open one of those small brown kraft paper bags and get to stuffing while we were oohing and aahing and huffing and puffing.

317
02:20:44,612 --> 02:20:51,115
[SPEAKER_06]: See, Mr. Henry had all the treats, all of our favorites, a hundred great sweets.

318
