[SPEAKER_06]: So, so, um,

357
02:23:16,715 --> 02:23:29,879
[SPEAKER_06]: I talk a lot, and my guys, by extension, talk a lot about this neighborhood, because when Jonathan and I got together, what are we talking, seven, eight years ago?

358
02:23:29,899 --> 02:23:42,483
[SPEAKER_06]: About seven years ago, he kind of came into the conversation based on stuff that he had heard I was doing, talking about our community.

359
02:23:44,910 --> 02:23:50,875
[SPEAKER_06]: So we based what we do on the premise that community is really, really important.

360
02:23:51,495 --> 02:23:57,520
[SPEAKER_06]: And then the next premise is that we met at the intersection of jazz and social justice.

361
02:23:57,620 --> 02:24:01,423
[SPEAKER_06]: So we're all about that life, as the kids like to say.

362
02:24:01,443 --> 02:24:06,346
[SPEAKER_06]: We're all about, you know, the music, obviously, but also the message.

363
02:24:06,426 --> 02:24:13,812
[SPEAKER_06]: So this is about community and about the way that community changes over time, and it's called Corner Lot.

364
02:24:42,484 --> 02:24:48,506
[SPEAKER_06]: standing at the apex of Arlington and Jerome, trying to remember the black and the brown and the tan.

365
02:24:49,667 --> 02:24:52,868
[SPEAKER_06]: Ronnie and Otis used to live in the big house on the corner lot.

366
02:24:53,608 --> 02:24:56,589
[SPEAKER_06]: It's probably changed a half dozen times since then.

367
02:24:57,529 --> 02:24:59,250
